1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,423
In October, India's central bank raised interest rates

2
00:00:03,423 --> 00:00:06,305
for the first time in more than three years.

3
00:00:06,305 --> 00:00:09,827
And in America, markets now expect the Federal Reserve

4
00:00:09,827 --> 00:00:11,620
to raise rates in December.

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:14,630
After years of cuts, rates are going up again.

6
00:00:14,630 --> 00:00:17,961
So what does that actually mean for your loan, your

7
00:00:17,961 --> 00:00:19,498
rent, and your savings?

8
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:22,680
Let's walk through it, in about 4 minutes.

9
00:00:23,167 --> 00:00:26,099
Think of a central bank as the bank for banks.

10
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:29,454
When your bank needs money overnight, it borrows at

11
00:00:29,454 --> 00:00:31,067
the central bank's rate.

12
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:35,002
In India, that's the repo rate, now 5.5%.

13
00:00:35,002 --> 00:00:38,292
In the United States, it's the federal funds rate,

14
00:00:38,292 --> 00:00:41,712
now between 3.75% and 4%.

15
00:00:41,712 --> 00:00:45,131
When that rate goes up, money becomes more expensive

16
00:00:45,131 --> 00:00:47,367
for every bank in the country.

17
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:51,588
So why would anyone make money more expensive on purpose?

18
00:00:51,588 --> 00:00:52,972
One word: inflation.

19
00:00:52,972 --> 00:00:56,662
When prices rise too fast, a central bank raises rates,

20
00:00:56,662 --> 00:01:00,155
so people borrow and spend a little less, and prices

21
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:00,879
cool down.

22
00:01:00,879 --> 00:01:05,360
This year, oil jumped above $100 a barrel during the

23
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:08,721
conflict in West Asia, and that pushed up the cost

24
00:01:08,721 --> 00:01:12,081
of almost everything that has to be made or moved.

25
00:01:12,081 --> 00:01:15,508
Central banks are trying to stop that from becoming

26
00:01:15,508 --> 00:01:16,467
permanent.

27
00:01:16,967 --> 00:01:18,028
Here's the chain.

28
00:01:18,028 --> 00:01:20,222
The central bank raises its rate.

29
00:01:20,222 --> 00:01:23,449
Banks now pay more to borrow, so they charge more

30
00:01:23,449 --> 00:01:24,030
to lend.

31
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:27,579
Floating-rate home loans, car loans and business loans

32
00:01:27,579 --> 00:01:28,482
reset higher.

33
00:01:28,482 --> 00:01:31,773
And companies paying more interest often pass some

34
00:01:31,773 --> 00:01:34,354
of it on in their prices, and in rents.

35
00:01:34,354 --> 00:01:37,839
It doesn't happen overnight, but within a few months,

36
00:01:37,839 --> 00:01:40,527
it shows up in your monthly payments.

37
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:42,497
Let's put a number on it.

38
00:01:42,497 --> 00:01:46,466
Take a $400,000 home loan, over 30 years.

39
00:01:46,466 --> 00:01:53,201
At 6.25%, the payment is about $2,463 a month.

40
00:01:53,201 --> 00:01:58,192
At 6.5%, it's about $2,528.

41
00:01:58,192 --> 00:02:02,161
Just a quarter of a percent adds $65, every single

42
00:02:02,161 --> 00:02:02,582
month.

43
00:02:02,582 --> 00:02:07,873
That's about $23,500 over the life of the loan.

44
00:02:08,367 --> 00:02:11,620
Now, many banks don't raise your payment at all.

45
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:14,700
Instead, they quietly make your loan longer.

46
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:18,533
Your EMI stays the same, but you pay for more months,

47
00:02:18,533 --> 00:02:20,791
and that usually costs far more.

48
00:02:20,791 --> 00:02:25,035
On a ₹50 lakh loan over 20 years, a quarter-point

49
00:02:25,035 --> 00:02:30,510
rise means paying about ₹780 more each month, around

50
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:32,974
₹1.9 lakh extra in total.

51
00:02:32,974 --> 00:02:36,601
Keep the same EMI instead, and the loan runs about

52
00:02:36,601 --> 00:02:39,407
a year longer, costing nearly ₹5 lakh.

53
00:02:39,407 --> 00:02:43,035
So if your bank extends your loan, ask about raising

54
00:02:43,035 --> 00:02:44,567
the EMI instead.

55
00:02:45,067 --> 00:02:46,584
There is good news too.

56
00:02:46,584 --> 00:02:50,494
When rates rise, banks usually pay more on fixed deposits

57
00:02:50,494 --> 00:02:51,370
and savings.

58
00:02:51,370 --> 00:02:54,875
If you have cash sitting idle, it's worth comparing

59
00:02:54,875 --> 00:02:56,156
deposit rates now.

60
00:02:56,156 --> 00:02:59,526
Just remember, a higher rate only really helps if

61
00:02:59,526 --> 00:03:01,107
it beats inflation.

62
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:04,721
So, rising rates mean dearer loans, a little relief

63
00:03:04,721 --> 00:03:08,252
for savers, and a good reason to check your own numbers.

64
00:03:08,252 --> 00:03:10,915
You can do that free at calcsouq.com.

65
00:03:10,915 --> 00:03:14,198
Our mortgage and EMI calculators show exactly what

66
00:03:14,198 --> 00:03:15,995
a rate change means for you.

67
00:03:15,995 --> 00:03:19,154
If this helped, subscribe for more explainers like

68
00:03:19,154 --> 00:03:19,773
this one.

69
00:03:19,773 --> 00:03:21,560
See you in the next one.
